## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| **Critical** | **2** |
| **Medium** | 2 |
| **Low** | 1 |

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** test class. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, achieving high line coverage, but almost nothing is actually verified. Bugs in pricing math, CSV parsing, search logic, and deletion would all pass these tests silently.

---

### 🔴 Critical

#### 1. Coverage-Touching — Systematic assertion-free tests (6 of 10 methods)

**Lines 17–21, 33–36, 39–43, 63–66, 69–74, 85–88**

Six test methods call production code and never assert *anything*. They exist only to make coverage tools colour lines green.

| Test Method | What it misses |
|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | Never verifies the product was actually stored |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33) | Never checks the price changed to 14.99 |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39) | Never checks the product is gone |
| `GetProductCount_Works` (L63) | Never asserts the count value |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L69) | Never checks prices were reduced by 10% |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85) | Never checks that the imported product exists |

**Fix — example for each (before → after):**

```csharp
// AddProduct_Works → AddProduct_StoresProduct
var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
_catalog.AddProduct(product);
var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNotNull(stored);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored.Price);

// UpdatePrice_Works → UpdatePrice_ChangesPrice
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);

// RemoveProduct_Works → RemoveProduct_MakesProductUnretrievable
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));

// GetProductCount_Works → GetProductCount_ReturnsZeroForEmptyCatalog
Assert.AreEqual(0, _catalog.GetProductCount());

// ApplyBulkDiscount_Works → ApplyBulkDiscount_ReducesPricesByFraction
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 10.00m));
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(9.00m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);

// ImportFromCsv_Works → ImportFromCsv_CreatesProduct
_catalog.ImportFromCsv("SKU-001,Widget,9.99");
var imported = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNotNull(imported);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", imported.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, imported.Price);
```

#### 2. Trivial null-check-only assertions (3 tests)

**Lines 28, 51, 81** — `GetProduct_Works`, `SearchProducts_Works`, `ExportToCsv_Works`

These tests do assert — but only `Assert.IsNotNull(result)`. They never check the *content* of the result. A `Search("Widget")` that returns the wrong products, or `ExportToCsv()` that emits garbage, would pass.

**Fix — example for `SearchProducts_Works`:**
```csharp
// Before
var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
Assert.IsNotNull(results);

// After
var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
Assert.AreEqual(1, results.Count);
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", results[0].Sku);
```

Similarly for `GetProduct_Works` (assert `Name`/`Price`) and `ExportToCsv_Works` (assert CSV content contains `"SKU-001,Widget,9.99"`).

